{
  "teamId": 108,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Los Angeles Angels arrived with tantalizing promise — a fully healthy Mike Trout alongside the incomparable two-way marvel Shohei Ohtani — and for the first month they delivered, racing out to a 14-8 April that had the fanbase daring to dream. Taylor Ward emerged as an unlikely offensive catalyst, slugging his way into the hearts of Angels fans with a string of clutch home runs, while Ohtani continued his otherworldly contributions on both the mound and at the plate. It felt, briefly, like the year the Angels would finally end their decade-long playoff drought.\n\nMay told a more complicated story. The offense remained dangerous — Ward and Ohtani delivering highlight-reel moments — but the bullpen began showing its cracks. Raisel Iglesias, counted on as the closer, blew critical leads in back-to-back appearances, surrendering walk-off shots that hemorrhaged wins the Angels could ill afford. A 13-15 May record exposed the fragility beneath the surface glamour, and the warning signs were unmistakable for anyone paying close attention.\n\nJune and July brought the collapse into full, painful view. The Angels went 10-18 in June and a catastrophic 6-18 in July — a stretch that rendered any postseason conversation moot before the summer was half over. The bullpen continued to betray the team at the worst moments, surrendering grand slams, three-run home runs, and late-inning leads with grim regularity. Injuries to key contributors compounded the misery, and the franchise once again found itself sellers rather than buyers as the trade deadline approached.\n\nAugust and September provided the bittersweet coda so familiar to Angels followers. Freed from expectations, the team played looser and better — 14-15 in August and 14-12 in September — as Ohtani reached 30 home runs and young players flashed potential. But the final record of 73-89 told the honest truth: another lost season, another October watching from home, and another offseason of hard questions for a franchise that had squandered perhaps its final years of Trout and Ohtani together.",
  "chapters": [
